WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
Three ghosts and a time machine. The ending&nbsp;
of "Better Call Saul" didn't go sci-fi on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.960
us – but it does need some explanation. From&nbsp;
that desperate escape to the last cigarette,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.160
here's everything you need to&nbsp;
know. Warning: spoilers ahead.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.480
By Better Call Saul's final episode,&nbsp;
Jimmy McGill has lived under three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.600
names and identities. He starts&nbsp;
as Jimmy, using his birth name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.200
and is a hardworking lawyer who seemingly&nbsp;
can't resist a shortcut or scheme.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.320
He then becomes Saul Goodman, aiding and abetting&nbsp;
the murderous rise of drug kingpin Walter White.
 &nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.680
Finally, we see him as Gene Takovic,&nbsp;
the manager of a shopping mall Cinnabon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.120
who lives in constant fear of his&nbsp;
previous identities being discovered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.960
The final episode, "Saul Gone," gives&nbsp;
each of them the ending they deserve.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.960
Gene Takovic died as he lived, hiding&nbsp;
from the police in a literal dumpster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.880
After months of hiding in Omaha, buried under&nbsp;
a thick mustache and even thicker glasses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.680
Gene dips his toe back into the grifter life after&nbsp;
being recognized by dim-witted taxi driver Jeff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.480
However, the erstwhile Slippin' Jimmy doesn't&nbsp;
do anything halfway. His schemes become riskier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.400
his true self comes through a little too much,&nbsp;
and before long his cover is completely blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.800
by Jeff's YouTube-savvy mother Marion,&nbsp;
played by comedy legend Carol Burnett.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.120
The final four episodes of the series primarily&nbsp;
take place in the Gene Takovic era. This came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.200
as something of a surprise to co-creator&nbsp;
Vince Gilligan, who told Rolling Stone,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.520
"We were fascinated by Jimmy McGill [...] and then&nbsp;
we wanted to see more of Gene Takovic in Omaha,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.960
so we kind of ran out the clock without&nbsp;
even meaning to, and then we realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.120
man, the first thing that could go was Saul&nbsp;
Goodman. And that's the name of the show!"

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.120
It's only fitting that a man who was once so good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.920
at elder law would finally be&nbsp;
brought to justice by an elder.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.600
Much like Gus Fring, Gene is both a career&nbsp;
criminal and a steadfast and reliable fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.680
food employee. Even while he's in the custody&nbsp;
of the Omaha police, Gene uses his phone call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.280
to contact Cinnabon and let his staff know&nbsp;
that he won't be coming in ever again.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.200
"This is how they get you,&nbsp;
this is how they get you."

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.080
Pacing in his holding cell like an animal, Gene&nbsp;
is furious with himself. After working as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.560
cartel lawyer for years, he is finally caught&nbsp;
because of a small-time identity theft scheme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.640
Lying on the cold floor, he glances up at&nbsp;
a crude carving on the cell wall that says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.600
"MY LAWYER WILL REAM UR ASS." Like a switch&nbsp;
being flipped, Gene Takovic dies and Saul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.600
Goodman is reborn. Gould, who wrote the&nbsp;
script, revealed that he views Gene, Saul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.120
and Jimmy as three very separate&nbsp;
characters, telling Rolling Stone,

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.120
"Since this is an episode where he goes from&nbsp;
Gene to Saul and, eventually, back to Jimmy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.400
I was pretty careful to use the name&nbsp;
that felt 'right.' at every moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.080
I called those moments out in the script."

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.680
After months of hiding from the world, Saul&nbsp;
Goodman returns. The world's greatest lawyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.600
finds himself sitting at a table across from&nbsp;
a battalion of hardened federal attorneys who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.920
are ready to put him away for life plus&nbsp;
190 years for working with Walter White.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.160
At his side is Bill Oakley, his favorite public&nbsp;
defender punching bag turned defense attorney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.880
but only as an advisor. Saul represents himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.280
and his defense is a doozy. With pleading eyes&nbsp;
and a trembling voice, he tells the collected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.520
prosecutors that he worked for Walter White&nbsp;
under fear of death. Lead prosecutor George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.480
Castellano asks if Saul really thinks a jury&nbsp;
will believe his story. Saul shrewdly responds,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.160
"All I need is one."

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.760
The scene reflects what many thought&nbsp;
the show would be from the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.240
playing on the character's comic relief and his&nbsp;
penchant for legal chicanery. "Better Call Saul"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.800
did have plenty of that, but few expected Jimmy&nbsp;
McGill to be such an emotionally rich character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.560
least of all the actor playing him. In&nbsp;
an interview with the Los Angeles Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.920
Bob Odenkirk described the start of his&nbsp;
journey playing the character, saying,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.520
"So that makes me, as a beginning actor, really&nbsp;
have to dig up a lot of stuff and feel a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.840
feelings from my own life in a visceral way that&nbsp;
left me feeling pretty shagged out and ragged.”

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.360
That scene where Saul lays out his "victim" angle&nbsp;
isn't all fun and games, of course. Saul is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.840
the hook for money laundering, bribery, and being&nbsp;
an accessory after the fact to dozens of murders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.640
including those of Hank Schrader and Steve Gomez.&nbsp;
On hand to represent those victims is Hank's wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.360
Marie Schrader, played by Betsy Brandt. The pain&nbsp;
of Hank's death clearly still weighs on Marie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.360
and she delivers a devastating monologue about&nbsp;
her husband. Saul's use of her real pain to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.440
pivot into his own false narrative is one of&nbsp;
his most callous moments in the entire series.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.160
"They tell me they found you in a&nbsp;
garbage dumpster. Well that makes sense."

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.000
This was Brandt's first appearance&nbsp;
on "Better Call Saul" and her first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.200
time playing Marie since the end of&nbsp;
"Breaking Bad" nine years earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.440
She joins several returning guest stars in the&nbsp;
finale, including Bryan Cranston as Walter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.920
and Michael McKean as Jimmy's brother Chuck. When&nbsp;
asked if there were any other actors or characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.520
from the "Better Call Saul" and "Breaking Bad"&nbsp;
universe he wanted to bring back for the finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.280
Gould's answer was essentially&nbsp;
everyone. As he told Rolling Stone,

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.960
"I would have loved to have Patrick&nbsp;
Fabian back, to have Dean Norris back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.560
Anna Gunn would have been great if it fit in&nbsp;
the story. I love our entire cast [...] I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.000
greedy. We didn't want to make a kind of&nbsp;
overstuffed epic, and I hope we didn't."

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.880
Just as Saul returns to the law&nbsp;
on his own behalf, so does Kim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.960
First, she delivers her notarized confession to&nbsp;
the district attorney and Howard's widow Cheryl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.440
Hamlin in the penultimate episode "Waterworks."&nbsp;
Then, she returns to her purposely bland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.160
indecisive life in central Florida. Still, the&nbsp;
act of confession has changed something in Kim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.480
and she takes a tentative step forward in&nbsp;
her life by turning up at a legal aid office.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.400
Kim's fate has been a driving question for the&nbsp;
series and its fans from the very beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.440
Kim was one of the few main characters&nbsp;
who did not appear in "Breaking Bad,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.520
and many fans feared the worst, thinking&nbsp;
Kim might be killed by the cartel. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.520
creator Peter Gould told Rolling Stone that it&nbsp;
was important that Kim's journey was her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.920
and not simply a detail in the Jimmy McGill story.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.320
In the finale, Saul returns to an Albuquerque&nbsp;
courtroom, once again wearing an outlandish suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.920
Viewers presume some big twist is coming,&nbsp;
that Saul has a final stunt to pull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.680
and that he might not end up serving the&nbsp;
seven-and-a-half year sentence he pled down to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.360
There is indeed a twist coming, but&nbsp;
not one that anyone could have guessed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.760
Saul begins the hearing, which is supposed to be&nbsp;
a formality to ratify his plea deal, by re-telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.760
the story of meeting Walter White for the first&nbsp;
time, fearing for his life out in the desert.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:46.560
Then he stops and, for once, tells the truth.&nbsp;
He kept working with Walt for 16 blood-soaked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.640
months because he smelled money. The scene also&nbsp;
invokes Jimmy's emotional speech in front of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.280
bar association at the end of Season 4, which&nbsp;
got his law license reinstated and inflicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.680
Saul Goodman upon the world. In both cases,&nbsp;
he is playing to his audience, using his real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.800
emotions to affect the desired outcome. But this&nbsp;
time, it's an audience of one, and that's Kim.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.920
"I just wanted her to come here&nbsp;
today. I wanted her to hear this."

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.880
Interspersed with the main story are three&nbsp;
flashbacks to earlier times in both "Better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.880
Call Saul" and "Breaking Bad." During these&nbsp;
moments, an important question is considered.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.840
"What would you do if you had a time machine?"

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:22.960
"A time machine?"

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.160
The first flashback takes us to the Season 5&nbsp;
episode "Bagman," when Jimmy and Mike face a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.840
harrowing two days in the Mexican desert hauling&nbsp;
$7 million in cash for Lalo Salamanca's bail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.920
Saul also poses the question to Walter White as&nbsp;
the two of them await being whisked away by Ed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.080
the vacuum repairman in a scene that takes place&nbsp;
during the "Breaking Bad" episode "Granite State."

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:47.200
"If I'm lucky, a month from now, best case&nbsp;
scenario, I'm managing a Cinnabon in Omaha."

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.040
Gould revealed that he wanted the flashbacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.800
to show how Jimmy is making a change&nbsp;
at long last, telling Rolling Stone,

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.720
"The feeling I had about this episode was that&nbsp;
it was a little bit like 'A Christmas Carol.'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.640
Gene becomes Saul, and he's visited by&nbsp;
three ghosts. And each time he's visited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:03.600
by one of these ghosts, you realize&nbsp;
this guy is trapped in the cycle."

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:09.280
While speaking to others, Jimmy's answers to the&nbsp;
time machine question are shallow and facile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.200
But his real answer is found in the episode's&nbsp;
third flashback, a moment from the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.120
beginning of "Better Call Saul," as Jimmy&nbsp;
delivers groceries to his brother Chuck.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.280
Chuck has taken a leave of absence from the law&nbsp;
firm he founded with Howard Hamlin's father due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.920
to a debilitating sensitivity to electromagnetic&nbsp;
waves. In this moment of brotherly kindness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.000
Chuck makes a small effort to reach out to&nbsp;
Jimmy, but the future Saul Goodman doesn't bite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.840
leaving Chuck to read H.G. Wells' "The&nbsp;
Time Machine" by himself in the dark.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.280
As much as the relationship between Jimmy&nbsp;
and Kim has come to define the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.280
for the first three seasons his relationship&nbsp;
with Chuck was arguably more important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.800
In an interview with the Los Angeles&nbsp;
Times, Odenkirk said of Jimmy,

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.400
"He's a guy who is showing the audience his&nbsp;
need, his hunger for love and respect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.760
his brother Chuck [...] His heart is&nbsp;
open to Chuck, and Chuck crushes him."

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:54.080
It's impossible not to wonder&nbsp;
how different Jimmy's life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.000
would have been had his brother still been alive.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.480
Jimmy's truth-telling stunt&nbsp;
gets him 86 years in prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.160
but it turns out the prison isn't such&nbsp;
a bad place for Saul Goodman to end up.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.160
"Better Call Saul."

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.080
For one, everyone calls him Saul, and he gets&nbsp;
fist bumps as he walks through the halls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.160
He even gets to nurture his Gene Takovic&nbsp;
side by baking bread in the prison cafeteria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.480
According to Gould, this was always the natural&nbsp;
endpoint for Jimmy, as he told Rolling Stone,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.160
"This guy has lived in the justice system,&nbsp;
he's made a farce of it, he's played it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:27.440
And it just felt like that was the right&nbsp;
place to end the series: him behind bars."

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.760
Jimmy may be behind bars, but he's&nbsp;
not alone, and Kim even visits him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.320
posing as his lawyer. The two lean up against&nbsp;
the back wall of the room and share a cigarette.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.320
It's a quiet, intimate scene that evokes a&nbsp;
scene the couple shared in the pilot episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.720
The cigarette burns in color against the scene's&nbsp;
film noir-inspired black and white filter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.200
Appropriately, this was the final scene that Rhea&nbsp;
Seehorn and Bob Odenkirk filmed together, and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:54.080
both the characters and the actors saying goodbye.&nbsp;
After the episode aired, Seehorn told Vulture,

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.720
"Oh, what a great gift when your&nbsp;
own emotions match up with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:58.240
your character is supposed to be feeling."

00:08:59.280 --> 00:08:59.920
"Hi Jimmy."

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.200
The saga ends with a wistful look and a pair&nbsp;
of finger guns from behind bars as Kim leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.360
Jimmy to serve his 86-year sentence.&nbsp;
Jimmy McGill goes out on his own terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:13.600
atoning for his crimes, and closer to the&nbsp;
woman he loves than he has been in years.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.200
Still, "Better Call Saul" leaves the&nbsp;
audience with many questions. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.720
will Cheryl go through with her plan to&nbsp;
sue Kim for her role in Howard's death?

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:23.680
"I could sue you in civil court, I&nbsp;
could take everything you've got."

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:28.880
Is Jesse doing better in Alaska than Gene&nbsp;
did in Omaha? Is this really the last time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.480
Jimmy and Kim will see each other? Will&nbsp;
Jimmy accept his prison fate so easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.480
or will the old Slippin' Jimmy come&nbsp;
creeping up? And are Jimmy's enemies dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.800
or is there some associate of the Salamancas that&nbsp;
is still out there with a thirst for revenge?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.648
We may never know the answers to these&nbsp;
questions, but it was still one hell of a finale.

